The '''Opportunity Party''', formerly known as '''the Opportunities Party''' ('''TOP'''), is a [[centrist]] [[List of political parties in New Zealand|political party in New Zealand]]. It was founded in 2016 by economist and philanthropist [[Gareth Morgan (economist)|Gareth Morgan]]. The party is based on the idea of [[evidence-based policy]] and being located in the [[political centre]]. It has contested three elections, and has not won any seats in parliament.

The party received 2.4% of the [[party vote]] in the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]] (after which Morgan resigned from the leadership), then 1.5% in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]]. It won an elected office for the first time in the [[2022 New Zealand local elections|2022 local elections]], with one member elected to the [[Featherston, New Zealand|Featherston]] Community Board in the [[South Wairarapa District]]. It received 2.2% of the party vote in the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], after which party leader [[Raf Manji]] stepped down.

In November 2025, businesswoman [[Qiulae Wong]] was announced as the party's new leader after a period of searching, and the party was rebranded to ''the Opportunity Party'' with a set of updated policy priorities.

==History==
===Formation===
The Opportunities Party was founded by economist and philanthropist [[Gareth Morgan (economist)|Gareth Morgan]], who launched it on 4 November 2016. On 10 January 2017 the party announced that it had 2,000 members and was applying for registration.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/88273022/gareth-morgan-registers-political-party-to-prepare-for-potential-early-election|title=Gareth Morgan registers political party to prepare for potential early election |last=Sachdeva |first=Sam |date=10 January 2017|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=11 January 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180831181625/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/88273022/gareth-morgan-registers-political-party-to-prepare-for-potential-early-election|url-status=live}}</ref> It was registered by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] on 6 March 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Register of political parties |url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/political-parties-in-new-zealand/register-of-political-parties/ |access-date=17 October 2022 |website=Elections |archive-date=1 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200601084740/https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/political-parties-in-new-zealand/register-of-political-parties/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The party's chief of staff [[Geoff Simmons]] contested the [[2017 Mount Albert by-election|Mount Albert by-election on 25 February 2017]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/88965792/gareth-morgans-party-to-take-on-jacinda-ardern-in-mt-albert-byelection |title=Gareth Morgan's party to take on Jacinda Ardern in Mt Albert by-election |last=Sachdeva|first=Sam|date=1 February 2017|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=1 February 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180831181606/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/88965792/gareth-morgans-party-to-take-on-jacinda-ardern-in-mt-albert-byelection |url-status=live}}</ref> The party was criticised by [[David Seymour (New Zealand politician)|David Seymour]] during the by-election for offering free bus trips for voters, which he asserted breached the Electoral Act.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/89522538/act-party-says-tops-offer-of-free-bus-rides-to-voters-breaks-the-law|title=ACT Party says TOP's offer of free bus rides to voters breaks the law|date=17 February 2017|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=18 February 2017|archive-date=31 August 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180831180119/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/89522538/act-party-says-tops-offer-of-free-bus-rides-to-voters-breaks-the-law |url-status=live}}</ref> The Electoral Commission cleared TOP of any wrongdoing.<ref>{{Cite web |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/89665632/the-opportunities-party-cleared-of-treating-after-giving-free-rides-to-voters |title=The Opportunities Party cleared of 'treating' after giving free rides to voters |date=21 February 2017 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=21 February 2017|archive-date=31 August 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180831181608/https://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/89665632/the-opportunities-party-cleared-of-treating-after-giving-free-rides-to-voters |url-status=live}}</ref> Simmons received 623 votes (4.56% of the total vote), placing him third.<ref>{{Cite web |url= https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/2017_mt_albert_byelection/ |title=Mt Albert – Official Result |website=www.electionresults.govt.nz |access-date=27 November 2019|archive-date=14 January 2020|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200114131849/https://electionresults.govt.nz/2017_mt_albert_byelection/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

===2017 general election===
[[File:TOP logo 2017.svg|thumb|Logo circa 2017]]
In May 2017, Morgan announced the party's first candidates for the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]],<ref>{{Cite news |url= http://www.top.org.nz/gareth_morgan_announces_the_opportunities_party_top_first_set_of_candidates |title=Gareth Morgan Announces The Opportunities Party (TOP) First Set of Candidates|work=TOP|access-date=31 May 2017 |archive-date=15 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221015024049/https://www.top.org.nz/gareth_morgan_announces_the_opportunities_party_top_first_set_of_candidates |url-status=dead}}</ref> and announced further candidates in the following months,<ref>{{Cite news |title=Gareth Morgan Announces The Opportunities Party (TOP) Second Set of Candidates |work=TOP |url=http://www.top.org.nz/gareth_morgan_announces_the_opportunities_party_top_second_set_of_candidates|access-date=3 June 2017|archive-date=17 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221017031230/https://www.top.org.nz/gareth_morgan_announces_the_opportunities_party_top_second_set_of_candidates|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Jones|first=Nicholas|date=15 June 2017 |title=Mika to stand in Auckland Central for Gareth Morgan's new party|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11876845|access-date=24 September 2017|archive-date=24 September 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170924045552/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11876845|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=Mika Haka |language=en|work=TOP |url=http://www.top.org.nz/mika_x |access-date=6 July 2017|archive-date=25 February 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180225004009/http://www.top.org.nz/mika_x |url-status=dead}}</ref> including former [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] candidate Teresa Moore who joined Geoff Simmons as co-deputy leader.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.top.org.nz/teresa_moore |title=Teresa Moore |work=TOP |access-date=9 September 2017 |language=en |archive-date=20 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180320161004/http://www.top.org.nz/teresa_moore |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/96234446/gareth-morgan-adds-new-deputy-coleader-of-top-releases-party-list |title=Gareth Morgan adds new deputy co-leader of TOP, releases party list |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=28 August 2017 |access-date=9 September 2017 |archive-date=27 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240727062621/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/96234446/gareth-morgan-adds-new-deputy-coleader-of-top-releases-party-list |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Party lists in the 2017 New Zealand general election#Other registered parties|TOP's party list]] had 26 candidates, of which 21 were also contesting electorates.<ref>{{cite web|title=Party and candidate lists for 2017 Election|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/party-and-candidate-lists-2017-election|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]]|access-date=7 October 2017|archive-date=7 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171007120507/http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/party-and-candidate-lists-2017-election|url-status=live}}</ref>

TOP took [[TVNZ]] to court after being excluded from its televised election debates, but lost the case.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manch|first=Thomas|date=9 September 2020|title=Election 2020: TOP furious as TVNZ includes Advance NZ, Māori Party in debate|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122704336/election-2020-top-furious-as-tvnz-includes-advance-nz-mori-party--in-debate|access-date=9 September 2020|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en|archive-date=6 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201006145458/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122704336/election-2020-top-furious-as-tvnz-includes-advance-nz-mori-party--in-debate |url-status=live}}</ref> Morgan faced controversy during the campaign for referring to Labour leader [[Jacinda Ardern]] as "[[lipstick on a pig]]", suggesting that the new leader had style but not substance.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11908121 |title=Storm erupts over Gareth Morgan's 'lipstick on a pig' tweet|date=20 August 2017|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=19 April 2020 |issn=1170-0777|archive-date=27 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190727091736/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11908121 |url-status=live}}</ref> He also faced backlash when he criticised the public for being sad over the death of Ardern's cat. This backlash included an email from TOP candidate Jenny Condie, who said, "It is not merely Gareth's comments themselves – these are a reflection of the culture that exists within the party. There is a mismatch between our policies and our culture: between what we say we want to accomplish and how we actually behave." In response, Morgan told Condie to resign from the party.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Rowe|first=Don|date=11 November 2017|title='Another day where it feels embarrassing to be associated with TOP': the email which enraged Morgan |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/11-11-2017/another-day-where-it-feels-embarrassing-to-be-associated-with-top-the-email-which-enraged-morgan/|access-date=14 September 2020|website=[[The Spinoff]] |archive-date=30 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201030194634/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/11-11-2017/another-day-where-it-feels-embarrassing-to-be-associated-with-top-the-email-which-enraged-morgan/|url-status=live}}</ref>

TOP gained 2.4% of the vote, winning no parliamentary seats.<ref name="2017 general election results">{{cite web |title=2017 General Election – Official Result |url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/ |access-date=7 October 2017 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]] |archive-date=7 October 2017 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20171007120051/http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Morgan vowed to continue fighting for a "fairer New Zealand" and maintained that TOP was not a failure as it was the fifth most popular party.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Swinnen|first1=Lucy|title=Party 'for a fairer New Zealand' falls flat, as Gareth Morgan's TOP falls far short of 5 per cent |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/97173822 |access-date=7 October 2017|publisher=[[Stuff.co.nz]]|date=24 September 2017|archive-date=27 August 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180827144054/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/97173822|url-status=live}}</ref>

====Post-election events ====
In December 2017, Morgan, Simmons and two candidates resigned  from their roles. Morgan said the party would contest the 2020 election but he would not lead it.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/99839970/gareth-morgan-wont-lead-top-into-2020-election |access-date=14 December 2017|title=TOP loses leader Gareth Morgan and three other candidates in matter of hours|date=14 December 2017 |publisher=[[Stuff.co.nz]]|archive-date=21 July 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190721063341/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/99839970/gareth-morgan-wont-lead-top-into-2020-election |url-status=live}}</ref> The following week, candidates [[Jessica Hammond Doube]] and Jenny Condie launched a splinter group from TOP with the placeholder name "Next Big Thing". They both attributed their low list rankings to their having raised questions over Morgan's controversial remarks during the election campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/100003864/extop-candidates-start-new-political-action-group |author=Cooke, Henry |access-date=19 December 2017 |title=Ex-TOP candidates start new political action group |date=19 December 2017 |publisher=[[Stuff.co.nz]]|archive-date=27 August 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180827144053/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/100003864/extop-candidates-start-new-political-action-group |url-status=live}}</ref> Condie would become a councillor for Wellington in 2019,<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 February 2020|title=Councillor Jenny Condie |url=http://wellington.govt.nz/your-council/mayor-and-councillors/councillors/jenny-condie |access-date=13 September 2020|website=Wellington City Council |archive-date=19 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200919225456/https://wellington.govt.nz/your-council/mayor-and-councillors/councillors/jenny-condie|url-status=dead}}</ref> while Jessica Hammond would return to TOP for the 2020 election,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Simmons|first=Geoff|date=23 January 2020|title=First Candidate Announcement for 2020 |url=https://www.top.org.nz/2020_candidate_announcement_jessica_hammond_doube|access-date=13 September 2020 |website=TOP |archive-date=1 October 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201001115519/https://www.top.org.nz/2020_candidate_announcement_jessica_hammond_doube |url-status=dead}}</ref> after a culture shift within the party during its rebuilding phase.

On 9 July 2018, Morgan announced that the party board had decided to cancel the party's registration as it lacked the time and resources to contest the 2020 election. In late July, Morgan and the board announced that he would reconsider the decision after receiving expressions of interest from people sympathetic to the party's goals. Morgan also indicated in a [[Facebook]] post that he was willing to fund candidates and leaders sympathetic to the goals of the party.{{Citation needed|date=September 2020}}

In August 2018, the party appointed a new board and Simmons was appointed interim leader. The new team embarked on a "Listening Tour" across the country to gauge supporter reaction and interest.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2018 |author=<!--Not stated--> |title=The Listening Tour: Workshop Results |url= https://www.top.org.nz/the_listening_tour_workshop_results |access-date=24 October 2024 |website=The Opportunities Party |archive-date=10 January 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190110073922/https://www.top.org.nz/the_listening_tour_workshop_results |url-status=dead}}</ref> In December 2018, an [[2018 Opportunities Party leadership election|internal leadership election]] was conducted, with candidates for leader being Simmons, [[Donna Pokere-Phillips]], Amy Stevens, Anthony Singh, and [[Jessica Hammond-Doube]]. An election was also held for the member-representative to the board. Simmons was elected leader<ref>{{Cite web|title=Parliamentary Leader Results |url= https://d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net/garethmorgan/pages/2441/attachments/original/1544240223/TOP-Parliamentary-Leader-Results.pdf?1544240223 |website=d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net|access-date=10 January 2019}}</ref> and Pokere-Phillips the member representative.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Member Representative Results |url= https://d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net/garethmorgan/pages/2441/attachments/original/1544240224/TOP-Member-Representative-Results.pdf?1544240224 |website=d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net|access-date=10 January 2019|archive-date=10 January 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190110074016/https://d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net/garethmorgan/pages/2441/attachments/original/1544240224/TOP-Member-Representative-Results.pdf?1544240224 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===2020 general election ===
{{See also|2020 New Zealand general election}}
[[File:TOP Teal - A vote for change - black.png|thumb|Logo circa 2020]]
After gathering enough support to restart the party and continuing to register with the Electorate Commission, a rebrand and relaunch was held in October 2019. In the brand launch speech, Simmons called for a universal basic income and advances in environment and housing, and to "break the Labour / National duopoly".<ref>{{cite web |title=TOP Brand Launch |url= https://www.top.org.nz/top_brand_launch |access-date=19 April 2020 |work=TOP |publisher=The Opportunities Party |date=8 October 2019 |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201008095011/https://www.top.org.nz/top_brand_launch |archive-date=8 October 2020}}</ref>

TOP nominated [[Candidates in the 2020 New Zealand general election by electorate|candidates in multiple electorates]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.top.org.nz/candidates-2020|access-date=19 April 2020|title=TOP Candidates|publisher=The Opportunities Party|date=20 March 2020|archive-date=1 May 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200501142914/https://www.top.org.nz/candidates-2020|url-status=dead}}</ref> The [[Ōhāriu (New Zealand electorate)|Ōhāriu electorate]] was a particular focus: their Ōhāriu candidate Jessica Hammond Doube said that TOP intended to pour resources into that campaign, as it was the only seat that the party believed it could win.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Manch|first=Thomas|date=13 June 2020|title=The battle for Ōhāriu: TOP is back, and it's hoping to spoil a fight between Labour and National|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/121806722/the-battle-for-hriu-top-is-back-and-its-hoping-to-spoil-a-fight-between-labour-and-national|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022151457/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/121806722/the-battle-for-hriu-top-is-back-and-its-hoping-to-spoil-a-fight-between-labour-and-national|archive-date=22 October 2020|access-date=16 June 2020|work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]}}</ref> She polled third with 4,443 votes, 18,494 behind the winner, the incumbent [[Greg O'Connor (politician)|Greg O'Connor]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-33.html |title=Ōhāriu – Official Result |publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission |access-date=25 October 2021 |archive-date=15 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231215230708/https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-33.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Simmons contested [[Rongotai (New Zealand electorate)|Rongotai]] and came fourth with 3,387 electorate votes out of 45,649.<ref>{{Cite news |last=MacManus |first=Joel|date=9 July 2020|title=TOP leader Geoff Simmons announces run in Rongotai |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122085573/top-leader-geoff-simmons-announces-run-in-rongotai |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201021144740/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122085573/top-leader-geoff-simmons-announces-run-in-rongotai |archive-date=21 October 2020|access-date=9 July 2020|work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]}}</ref> At the beginning of his campaign he misspelt the electorate name as "Rongatai" in his advertising.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/the-opportunities-partys-geoff-simmons-misspells-electorates-name-in-advertising-campaign/UPZURX2C23KEANPK63T4FEH4DI/ |title=The Opportunities Party's Geoff Simmons misspells electorate's name in advertising campaign |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=24 September 2020 |access-date=8 November 2022 |archive-date=8 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221108071812/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/the-opportunities-partys-geoff-simmons-misspells-electorates-name-in-advertising-campaign/UPZURX2C23KEANPK63T4FEH4DI/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In July 2020, TOP's [[Auckland Central (New Zealand electorate)|Auckland Central]] candidate, Auckland café owner [[Joshua Love]], left the party following a dispute over his conduct and campaign activities. A warning letter from party secretary Ray McKeown raised concerns about compliance with [[Electoral Act 1993|Electoral Act]] requirements for candidate advertising and Love's behaviour towards party members. An unnamed TOP board member alleged that Love had attended a meeting while hungover and behaved aggressively and confrontationally. Love disputed that account, while apologising for making the board member feel unsafe.<ref name="Braae">{{cite news |last=Braae |first=Alex |title=Explosive start for Opportunities Party campaign as candidate takes dispute public |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/01-07-2020/explosive-start-for-opportunities-party-campaign-as-candidate-takes-dispute-public |work=The Spinoff |date=1 July 2020 |access-date=27 August 2026}}</ref> Shortly after his departure, an unidentified 20-year-old woman told ''[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]'' that Love had asked her to send him naked photographs after she contacted him to offer support.<ref name="StuffPhotos">{{cite news |title=Young woman disappointed after former The Opportunities Party candidate asks her for naked photographs |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/350540132/young-woman-disappointed-after-former-the-opportunities-party-candidate-asks-her-for-naked-photographs |work=Stuff |access-date=27 August 2026}}</ref> Love subsequently stood in Auckland Central as an independent, and TOP selected former [[New Zealand First]] MP [[Tuariki Delamere]] as its replacement candidate.<ref>{{cite news |last=Perich |first=Bronson |title=Auckland 'super city' a disaster imposed by Rodney Hyde – Tuariki Delamere |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2020/07/20/auckland-super-city-a-disaster-imposed-by-rodney-hyde-tuariki-delamere/ |work=Te Ao Māori News |date=20 July 2020 |access-date=27 August 2026}}</ref>

TOP won 43,449 party votes, 1.5% of the total.<ref name="results2020">{{cite web |date=6 November 2020 |title=2020 General Election and Referendums – Official Result |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/ |access-date=6 November 2020 |website=Electoral Commission |archive-date=23 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230723114355/https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Simmons resigned as leader after preliminary results were released, and [[Shai Navot]] became interim leader.<ref name=":1">{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/123287891/the-opportunities-party-leader-geoff-simmons-resigns-interim-leader-named |title=The Opportunities Party leader Geoff Simmons resigns, interim leader named |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=3 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201103070047/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/123287891/the-opportunities-party-leader-geoff-simmons-resigns-interim-leader-named| archive-date=3 November 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> On 27 January 2022, former Christchurch City Councillor and independent candidate for [[Ilam (New Zealand electorate)|Ilam]], [[Raf Manji]], was announced as the new leader.<ref name="StuffmanjiL">{{cite web |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127610888/raf-manji-former-christchurch-city-councillor-named-as-tops-new-leader |title=Raf Manji, former Christchurch city councillor named as TOP's new leader |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=27 January 2022 |access-date=27 January 2022 |archive-date=27 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220127000634/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127610888/raf-manji-former-christchurch-city-councillor-named-as-tops-new-leader |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=27 October 2022 |title=Listen: TOP's third leader joins Gone by Lunchtime |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/27-10-2022/listen-tops-third-leader-joins-gone-by-lunchtime |access-date=27 October 2022 |website=[[The Spinoff]] |archive-date=27 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221027053635/https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/27-10-2022/listen-tops-third-leader-joins-gone-by-lunchtime |url-status=live }}</ref>

===2022 local elections===
The party fielded four candidates in the [[2022 New Zealand local elections|2022 local elections]], the first time they had contested local elections. They had their first elected representative in John Dennison, who won a seat on the Featherston Community Board in the [[South Wairarapa District]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Starting at the bottom: Elected TOP candidate wins party milestone |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/10/16/starting-at-the-bottom-elected-top-candidate-wins-party-milestone/ |website=1 News |access-date=16 October 2022}}</ref>

=== 2023 general election ===
[[File:TOP CMYK Teal.png|thumb|Logo circa 2023]]
As of May 2023, TOP was averaging around 1–2% in opinion polls. Its leader, Raf Manji, said that realistically it would need to win the [[Ilam (electorate)|Ilam]] electorate to enter Parliament in the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]]. He had contested the electorate as an independent in 2017, coming second.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Election 2023: TOP bets its future on Ilam – can it win? |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/05/28/election-2023-top-bets-its-future-on-ilam-can-it-win/ |access-date=1 June 2023 |website=1 News |archive-date=1 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601070057/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/05/28/election-2023-top-bets-its-future-on-ilam-can-it-win/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On 4 August 2023, TOP announced their party list where they put forward 13 electorate candidates throughout New Zealand, and announced [[Natalia Albert]] as the new deputy leader.<ref name="fresh_team">{{cite web |last=Manji |first=Raf |date=4 August 2023 |title=The Opportunities Party announces a fresh team to bring new ideas to Parliament at the 2023 General Election |url=https://www.top.org.nz/the_opportunities_party_announces_a_fresh_team_to_bring_new_ideas_to_parliament_at_the_2023_general_election |website=TOP |access-date=3 August 2023 |archive-date=3 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230803223422/https://www.top.org.nz/the_opportunities_party_announces_a_fresh_team_to_bring_new_ideas_to_parliament_at_the_2023_general_election |url-status=dead }}</ref>

TOP received 2.22% of the party vote. It did not win any electorates or meet the five percent threshold needed to enter Parliament.<ref>{{cite web |title=2023 General Election – Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/index.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=11 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231125022036/https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/index.html |archive-date=25 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023}}</ref>  Manji came second in Ilam, with 10,863 votes, against [[New Zealand National Party|National]] candidate [[Hamish Campbell]]'s 18,693 votes.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ilam – Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-15.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=4 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123104115/https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-15.html |archive-date=23 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> Manji stepped down as leader in December 2023.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/350123529/top-leader-raf-manji-resigns |title=TOP leader Raf Manji resigns |date=3 December 2023 |work=The Post |first=Sinead |last=Gill |access-date=3 December 2023 |archive-date=3 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231203044629/https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/350123529/top-leader-raf-manji-resigns |url-status=live }}</ref> In August 2025 the party's general manager, former Labour MP and minister [[Iain Lees-Galloway]], advertised the party leader role on employment website [[SEEK]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360792451/leaderless-political-party-thats-turned-seek |title=The leaderless political party that's turned to SEEK |date=15 August 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |first=Lloyd |last=Burr |access-date=15 August 2025 }}</ref>

=== 2026 general election ===
In November 2025 the party chose [[Qiulae Wong|Qiulae 'Q' Wong]] to be their new leader.<ref name=":0"></ref> Wong used to work at [[KPMG]] as an adviser on sustainable business,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Opportunity: TOP rebrands as new leader chases 5% threshold |url= https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/11/16/opportunity-top-rebrands-as-new-leader-chases-5-threshold/ |access-date=16 November 2025 |website=1News}}</ref> and is the former director of the New Zealand branch of [[B Lab|B-Lab]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2023 |title=A decade of B-Corp. Now what? Qiulae Wong, NZ manager of B Lab |url=https://www.podcasts.nz/a-decade-of-b-corp-now-what-qiulae-wong-nz-manager-of-b-lab/ |access-date=15 November 2025 |website=Podcasts NZ: New Zealand Podcasts}}</ref> The party announced it would contest the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]] with a range of policies that include reducing house prices through a land value tax, and introducing a "Citizen's Voice", consisting of [[Citizens' assembly|citizens' assemblies]] for certain major issues.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Platform |url=https://www.opportunity.org.nz/platform |access-date=14 January 2026 |website=The Opportunity Party}}</ref> On 16 February, general manager [[Iain Lees-Galloway]] announced that the party would run candidates in 30 electorates. Wong confirmed she would contest the [[Mount Albert (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Albert electorate]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schmidt |first1=Veronica |title=Opportunity Party to double candidates this election, Qiulae Wong to stand in Mt Albert |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-02-2026/opportunity-party-to-double-candidates-this-election-qiulae-wong-to-stand-in-mt-albert |access-date=22 February 2026 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=16 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260216180409/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-02-2026/opportunity-party-to-double-candidates-this-election-qiulae-wong-to-stand-in-mt-albert |archive-date=16 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== Key policies ====

The party proposes three main reforms of the taxation system: introducing a [[citizen's income]], a [[land value tax]], and compulsory membership in the [[KiwiSaver]] savings scheme. The citizen's income (a version of a [[Universal basic income|UBI]]) would entail almost all adults getting paid [[New Zealand dollar|$]]370 a week, although people earning more than NZ$350,000 a year would not be able to access it.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Edmunds |first1=Susan |title=Ditch NZ Super entirely, minor party says |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586825/ditch-nz-super-entirely-minor-party-says |access-date=14 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260213065627/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586825/ditch-nz-super-entirely-minor-party-says |archive-date=13 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>  

The citizen's income would replace many [[Welfare spending|state welfare benefits]], including the unemployment benefit. The party says this will save millions in bureaucracy and will give people dignity.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |date =30 June 2026 |title= Explainer: What is the Opportunity Party and what are its policies? |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/642464/explainer-what-is-the-opportunity-party-and-what-are-its-policies |access-date =30 June 2026 |website =RNZ}}</ref> However, the figure of NZ$370 a week has been criticised as a "poverty-level payment" and as an amount that "will not be enough for most people to survive on".<ref>{{cite web |last1= Peters |first1= Tom |last2= Braddock |first2= John |date= 2 June 2026 |title= The right-wing politics of New Zealand's Opportunity Party |url= https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/06/03/jhau-j03.html |website= World Socialist Web Site |access-date= 16 August 2026}}</ref> The party estimates that 70% of New Zealanders would be net positive in this scenario, 20% net neutral, and 10% would end up paying more tax. The party believes that this would disincentivize the [[property market]] and would redirect wealth to productive parts of the economy.<ref>{{Cite web |title=It's time for a tax reset |publisher= Opportunity Party |url= https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KgTXUgjVipAA7EcDas-EJmOr6ZkeCf9B/view |access-date =11 May 2026 |via =Google Docs}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Russell |first =Alexia |date=8 May 2026 |title=The Opportunity Party: The election dark horse |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/594584/the-opportunity-party-the-election-dark-horse |access-date =11 May 2026 |website=RNZ}}</ref> 

The party also want to boost [[renewable energy]] through long-term cross-party support, increasing it by 30GW before 2050; and would merge energy regulatory agencies and consolidate the distributors down to eight.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Healthy Oceans |publisher= Opportunity Party |url= https://drive.google.com/file/d/1V8TIJAxJ2EYV0vYtVewo1co4ndE6eGTq/view |access-date=11 May 2026 |via=Google Docs}}{{primary source inline|date=July 2026}}</ref><ref name=":4" />

During the party's State of the Nation address in Auckland on 21 February 2026, Wong announced that the party would also campaign on promoting national unity, and banning [[bottom trawling]].<ref>{{cite web |author1=Opportunity Party |title='We Are The Next Generation Of Leaders' Says Qiulae Wong |url=https://m.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2602/S00118/we-are-the-next-generation-of-leaders-says-qiulae-wong.htm |website=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |access-date=22 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260221035522/https://m.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2602/S00118/we-are-the-next-generation-of-leaders-says-qiulae-wong.htm |archive-date=21 February 2026 |date=21 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In early July 2026, the party released its economic policy, which included investing in technology and commerce, strengthening the [[Commerce Commission]]'s powers and forgiving [[student loan]] interest in order to draw back emigrant New Zealanders.<ref>{{cite news |title=TOP unveils new economic policy |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/heather-du-plessis-allan-drive/audio/qiulae-wong-top-leader-on-the-party-announcing-new-economic-policy/ |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=2 July 2026}}</ref>

In August 2026, the party announced a policy to "clean up politics". This would involve regulating [[lobbying]] with a national database, implementing a year-long cooldown period before ministers and officials can become lobbyists, and setting stricter rules around conduct. The party would also reform [[Campaign finance|political donations]] by introducing a cap of $30,000 per donor for a party in each electoral cycle and lowering the threshold of disclosing the donor to $1,000.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=16 August 2026 |title=Opportunity Party vows to 'clean up politics' with lobbying and donations reforms |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1010243/opportunity-party-vows-to-clean-up-politics-with-lobbying-and-donations-reforms |access-date=16 August 2026 |website=[[Radio New Zealand]] }}</ref> The party claims that the donor cap is the figure recommended by the Independent Electoral Review.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Clean-up Politics |url=https://www.opportunity.org.nz/clean_up_politics |access-date=16 August 2026 |website=Opportunity Party }}</ref>

In mid-September 2026, Opportunity released its climate change policy, which included introducing a [[methane]] emissions trading scheme and investing $5 billion in [[climate adaptation]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Newton |first1=Kate |title=Opportunity proposes methane trading scheme, $5b climate adaptation plan |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/environment_climate/1453264/opportunity-proposes-methane-trading-scheme-5b-climate-adaptation-plan |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref>

==== Polling ====

During the campaign, [[Opinion polling for the 2026 New Zealand general election|opinion polls]] showed support for the party rising above 5% – the threshold for entering parliament – for the first time.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shaw |first=Richard |date=7 July 2026 |title=This 'centrist' party is rising in NZ polls. Will it prove power broker? |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/683812/this-centrist-party-is-rising-in-nz-polls-will-it-prove-power-broker |website= [[Radio New Zealand]]}}</ref> In August 2026, the party's support reached highs of 8%<ref>{{cite news |first= Justin |last=Hu |date=12 August 2026 |title= Poll: Opportunity Party surges as NZ First slides amid Luxon saga |url= https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/13/poll-opportunity-party-surges-as-nz-first-slides-amid-luxon-saga/ |work=1News}}</ref> and peaked at 9.5% in one Roy Morgan poll,<ref>{{cite web | title=Roy Morgan New Zealand Poll: In August National-led Government leads Labour-led Parliamentary Opposition (49% vs. 41%), Opportunity Party support rises again to a record high of 9.5% |publisher= Roy Morgan Research |date=31 August 2026 |url= https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10321-nz-national-voting-intention-august-2026 }}</ref> but dropped back to 4.5% in September's Taxpayers Union Poll.<ref> [https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1278458/coalition-returned-to-government-in-new-poll-as-opportunity-loses-kingmaker-spot Coalition returned to government in new poll, as Opportunity loses kingmaker spot], RNZ, 7 September 2026</ref> The party is open to a coalition with either Labour or National, with Wong saying they would begin negotiations with whichever party received the most votes. She subsequently qualified her party's position by stating that it would not support a government that had [[Winston Peters]] as foreign minister.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Sam |date=2 July 2026 |title=The Opportunity Party 'would be happy' to go into coalition with National |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361001030/opportunity-party-would-be-happy-go-coalition-national |access-date=14 July 2026 |website=Stuff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Chen |first=Liu |date=3 July 2026 |title=Chinese voters reconsider allegiances after Peters' 'racist' remarks |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/chinese_english/874066/chinese-voters-reconsider-allegiances-after-peters-racist-remarks |access-date=3 July 2026 |website=[[RNZ]]}}</ref> On 27 July, the National Party ruled out working with Opportunity, with their leader [[Christopher Luxon]] stating: "We wouldn't be in business with people who want to increase taxes."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dexter |first=Giles |date=27 July 2026 |title=National rules out working with Opportunity Party |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/809555/national-rules-out-working-with-opportunity-party |access-date=27 July 2026 |website=[[Radio New Zealand]] }}</ref>

In a poll conducted from 3 July to 13 August 2026, Talbot Mills tracked where the party's supporters came from in terms of how they voted at the 2023 general election, finding that about a third came from the left and about a third from the right, while a quarter had supported the party in that election.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Young |first1=Audrey |last2=Knox |first2=Chris |date=29 August 2026 |title=Your ultimate guide to Opportunity Party – as Qiulae Wong eyes potential balance-of-power role |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/your-ultimate-guide-to-opportunity-party-as-qiulae-wong-eyes-potential-balance-of-power-role/premium/NBNHFI3IZBCHHLNR2CQNNY37TE/ |access-date=29 August 2026 |website=[[New Zealand Herald]]}}</ref>

